WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.840
[Music]

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.960
I'm Jasmine Lawrence and I'm a program manager.

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:11.984
on the Xbox One engineering team.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:25.248
the world and we rely on the internet to make that happen. This is no easy task

00:00:25.248 --> 00:00:29.928
and there are a lot of things happening behind the scenes.

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:33.713
The internet is totally changing how people interact and connect.

00:00:33.713 --> 00:00:36.033
But how does it work?

00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:39.913
How do the computers all across the world actually communicate with each other?

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.697
Let's look at web browsing.

00:00:43.697 --> 00:00:45.857
First you open a web browser.

00:00:45.857 --> 00:00:49.257
It's the app you use to access the web pages.

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:49.777
Next you open a web browser.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:55.122
You type in the web address or URL, which stands for Uniform Resource Locator,

00:00:55.122 --> 00:00:59.682
of the website you want to visit, like tumblr.com.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:02.487
(upbeat music)

00:01:04.347 --> 00:01:06.547
Hi, I'm David Karp, the founder of Tumblr,

00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:07.707
and we're here today to talk about

00:01:07.707 --> 00:01:09.867
how those web browsers we use every day.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:19.851
actually work.

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:24.876
At the moment, your computer starts talking to another computer called a server that's

00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:27.636
usually thousands of miles away.

00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:29.860
And in milliseconds, your computer asks that.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.540
server for a website and that server starts to talk back to your computer in

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.845
a language called HTTP and HTTP stands for hypertext transfer.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:40.845
for protocol.

00:01:40.845 --> 00:01:44.725
You can kind of think of it as the language that one computer uses to ask another computer

00:01:44.725 --> 00:01:45.725
for a document.

00:01:45.725 --> 00:01:48.005
It's actually really pretty straightforward.

00:01:48.005 --> 00:01:49.829
If you were to intercept the conversation, you'd have to do it.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:52.829
between your computer and a web server on the internet.

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:55.829
It's mainly made up of something called GET requests.

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:57.829
And those are really very simply the word GET

00:01:57.829 --> 00:01:59.814
and then the name of the document that you're requesting.

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:09.799
So if you're trying to log into Tumblr and load our login page, all you're doing is sending a GET request to Tumblr server that says GET/LOGIN.

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:16.359
and that tells Tumblr server that you want all of the HTML code for the Tumblr login page.

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:19.639
So HTML stands for hypertext.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:25.783
markup language and you can think of that as the language that you use to tell a web browser how to make a page look.

00:02:25.783 --> 00:02:29.768
So if you think about something like Wikipedia, which is really just a big simple document

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:36.168
And HTML is the language that you use to make that title big and bold, to make the font the right font,

00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:39.748
to link certain text to

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:44.532
certain other pages to make some text bold, to make some text italic, to put an image

00:02:44.532 --> 00:02:47.772
in the middle of the page, to align the image to the right, to align the image to the left.

00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:56.137
webpage is included directly in the HTML, but other parts, images or videos, are separate

00:02:56.137 --> 00:02:59.297
files with their own URLs that need to be requested.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:05.721
The browser sends separate HTTP requests for each of these and displays them as they arrive.

00:03:07.721 --> 00:03:09.561
If a web page has a lot of different

00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:18.586
images, each of them causes a separate HTTP request and the page loads slower.

00:03:18.586 --> 00:03:19.691
Now sometimes when you browse

00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:24.131
browser web, you're not just requesting pages with get requests.

00:03:24.131 --> 00:03:29.571
Sometimes you send information, like when you fill out a form or type a search query.

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:36.675
browser sends this information in plain text to the web server using an HTTP post request.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:46.380
make a post request that is a post to Tumblr's login page that has some data attached to

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.700
it. It has your email address. It has your password.

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:54.524
to Tumblr server, Tumblr server figures out that, okay, you're David.

00:03:54.524 --> 00:03:59.004
It sends a webpage back to your browser that says, success, logged in as David.

00:03:59.004 --> 00:03:59.604
But along with that,

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:04.269
webpage it also attaches a little bit of invisible cookie data that your browser

00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:08.589
sees and knows to save and that's really important because it's really the

00:04:08.589 --> 00:04:09.549
only way that a website can

00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:12.014
can remember who you are.

00:04:12.014 --> 00:04:15.774
And all that cookie data really is an ID card for Tumblr.

00:04:15.774 --> 00:04:18.574
It's a number that identifies you as David.

00:04:18.574 --> 00:04:19.598
And your web browser holds--

00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:24.998
on to that number and then the next time you refresh tumblr, the next time you go to tumblr.com,

00:04:24.998 --> 00:04:29.583
your web browser knows to automatically attach that ID number with the request that it sent

00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:33.583
over to Tumblr server. So now Tumblr server sees the request coming from your browser,

00:04:33.583 --> 00:04:37.583
sees the ID number, and knows, "Okay, this is a request from David."

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.647
Now, the internet is completely open.

00:04:43.647 --> 00:04:48.407
All of its connections are shared, and information is sent in plain text.

00:04:48.407 --> 00:04:49.527
This makes it possible for how to use it.

00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:55.192
hackers to snoop on any personal information that you send over the internet.

00:04:55.192 --> 00:04:59.536
But safe websites prevent this by asking your web browser to communicate

00:04:59.536 --> 00:05:05.836
on a secure channel using something called Secure Socket Layer and its successor, Transport

00:05:05.836 --> 00:05:07.636
Layer Security.

00:05:07.636 --> 00:05:09.521
You can think of SSL and T-

00:05:09.521 --> 00:05:16.261
TLS as a layer of security wrapped around your communications to protect them from snooping or tampering.

00:05:17.201 --> 00:05:19.201
SSL and TLS are active.

00:05:19.506 --> 00:05:25.586
when you see the little block that appears in your browser address bar next to the HTTPS.

00:05:25.586 --> 00:05:29.486
The HTTPS protocols ensure that your HTTP requests are

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:39.475
secure and protected.

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:44.475
an official ID card, proving that it's the website it claims to be.

00:05:44.475 --> 00:05:49.235
Digital certificates are published by certificate authorities, which are trusted entities that

00:05:49.235 --> 00:05:49.435
vary.

00:05:49.459 --> 00:05:54.139
the identities of websites and issue certificates for them, just like a

00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:59.339
government can issue IDs or passports. Now if a website tries to start a secure

00:05:59.444 --> 00:06:07.444
connection without a properly issued digital certificate, your browser will warn you.

00:06:07.444 --> 00:06:09.124
That's the basics of web browsing.

00:06:09.428 --> 00:06:12.028
the part of the internet we see day to day.

00:06:12.528 --> 00:06:19.028
To summarize, HTTP and DNS manage the sending and receiving of HTML,

00:06:19.128 --> 00:06:19.413
meet the end of the web.

00:06:19.413 --> 00:06:25.353
files or anything on the web. What makes this possible under the hood are TCP/IP

00:06:25.353 --> 00:06:29.398
and router networks that break down and transport information

00:06:29.398 --> 00:06:31.398
in small packets.

00:06:31.398 --> 00:06:34.398
Those packets themselves are made up of binary.

00:06:34.398 --> 00:06:36.918
Sequences of ones and zeros that are physically sent through electric wires, fiber, and

00:06:39.382 --> 00:06:42.942
dropped at cables and wireless networks.

00:06:42.942 --> 00:06:46.782
Fortunately, once you've learned how one layer of the internet works, you can rely

00:06:46.782 --> 00:06:49.342
on it without remembering all the details.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:57.367
And we can trust that all those layers will work together to successfully deliver information at scale and with reliability.

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:01.931
(upbeat music)
